```grf:context id="quickstart"
{
  "format": "grf.public-document/v1",
  "title": "QUICKSTART",
  "content": "Open cloud quickstart ? Python 3.11+\n\n1. Extract the verified release ZIP, then:\npython verify_release.py\npython grf_starter.py init my-project --intent \"Build and verify my application\" --cloud\ncd my-project\n\n2. Start a concrete task:\npython grf.py session sign-in --task \"Implement the feature and verify its behavior\"\n\n3. Replace checks/acceptance.py with assertions that exercise your actual application. The initial deliberately failing check must not be presented as validation. Configure additional checks in project.grf as needed.\npython grf.py check\n\n4. After passing checks, save nonempty evidence inside the project, then:\npython grf.py session complete --summary \"Implemented and verified the requested behavior\" --evidence evidence/result.json\npython grf.py session sign-out\n\nExisting task after interruption:\npython grf.py session resume\n\nCloud enrollment happens automatically with --cloud. If setup was interrupted, rerun python grf_enroll.py my-project from the extracted release directory. Do not rerun init over an existing directory. Registration is per project; no owner approval for each command is required. Only exact action hashes are sent; local checks and output stay local. An unavailable/denying cloud service prevents checked commands from launching.\n\nFor local-only use, omit --cloud. To enable cloud later, run grf_enroll.py against the project. Older pinned projects must be deliberately upgraded after preserving their active state; use a fresh 0.5.1 project for the simplest safe start.\n\nPublic admission is cooperative and user-registered, not independent verification of test truth or command safety. Passing local checks remains the user's responsibility. Receipts retain local output, so checks must not print secrets. No daemon is installed. See README.grf for credential expiry, initial service limits and process-lifetime boundaries.\n"
}
```
